[THOUGHT] The proposed diagnosis is “Viral pericarditis”. While the clinical picture — pleuritic chest pain, fever, pericardial effusion, ECG changes (low-voltage QRS, R-wave fluctuation), and absence of alternative explanations (e.g., PE, HF, MI) — strongly supports *acute pericarditis*, the attribution to *viral etiology* remains presumptive. Critical evidence required to confirm or support viral cause includes:  
- Elevated inflammatory markers (CRP/ESR) — not yet reported;  
- Negative troponin I — needed to help distinguish isolated pericarditis from myopericarditis (which may alter management and prognosis);  
- Viral serologies or PCR (e.g., coxsackievirus, EBV, CMV, SARS-CoV-2) — not yet obtained;  
- Pericardial fluid analysis — not performed (and would be definitive if effusion is tapped);  
- Cardiac MRI — not done, though not mandatory acutely, it adds specificity for myocardial involvement/inflammation.  

Importantly, the case summary states these tests are *recommended* but does not confirm they have been completed. With only 1 turn remaining (15/16), we cannot assume pending labs have returned or that imaging has been repeated. Therefore, while “acute pericarditis” is well-supported, “viral pericarditis” as a *confirmed* diagnosis lacks direct virologic or serologic validation — and remains a clinical inference. Per guidelines (e.g., ESC 2015/2023 Pericarditis Guidelines), etiologic diagnosis (viral vs. autoimmune vs. idiopathic) often requires exclusion of mimics *and* supportive lab/imaging data — not just clinical pattern.

No red-flag omissions preclude *acute pericarditis* as the primary diagnosis — but labeling it definitively “viral” without supporting evidence exceeds diagnostic sufficiency at this stage.

[DECISION] INCOMPLETE

[FEEDBACK] Confirmatory evidence required before finalizing “viral pericarditis”: (1) Troponin I (to exclude myopericarditis), (2) CRP and/or ESR (to confirm systemic inflammation), and (3) at least one viral test (e.g., serum PCR or IgM for common cardiotropic viruses, or pericardial fluid PCR if pericardiocentesis performed). Without these, the etiology remains presumptive — the diagnosis should be downgraded to “acute pericarditis, likely viral” or “idiopathic” until data are available.

